\label #'Preface

\markup {
  \vspace #1
}

\markup \fill-line {
  \center-column \abs-fontsize #14 \smallCaps \bold {
    "Preface"
    \null
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #1
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    The six Partitas BWV 825–830 were the first music Bach published at his own expense. Starting in
    1726, three years after his appointment as Thomaskantor in Leipzig, he issued them one at a time,
    roughly one a year: Partita I in 1726, dedicated to the newborn prince Emanuel Ludwig of
    Anhalt-Köthen, Partitas II and III in 1727, Partita IV in 1728 and Partitas V and VI in 1730. In
    1731 he gathered them in a single volume, which he called his Opus 1: \italic { Clavier-Übung,
    bestehend in Praeludien, Allemanden, Couranten, Sarabanden, Giguen, Menuetten und andern
    Galanterien; denen Liebhabern zur Gemüths-Ergötzung verfertiget } (“Keyboard practice, consisting
    of preludes, allemandes, courantes, sarabandes, gigues, minuets and other galanteries, composed for
    music lovers to refresh their spirits”). Early versions of Partitas III and VI appear in the 1725
    \italic { Notebook for Anna Magdalena Bach. }
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #1
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    The word \italic { partita } at first meant a variation, as in Frescobaldi's \italic { partite } on
    a bass. In Germany it came to mean a suite of dances, and Bach's title deliberately recalls the
    \italic { Neue Clavier-Übung } (1689–1692) of Johann Kuhnau, his predecessor at the Thomasschule,
    whose collections also consist of partitas. The core of each suite is the sequence of allemande,
    courante, sarabande and gigue established by Froberger in the seventeenth century; between the
    sarabande and the gigue Bach inserts lighter pieces, the “galanteries”. Each partita opens with a
    different kind of prelude, so that the collection also surveys the introductory forms of the time:
    the Praeludium of Partita I, the Sinfonia of Partita II, which joins a slow French opening to an
    Andante and a two-part fugue, the Fantasia of Partita III, close to a two-part invention, the
    French Ouverture of Partita IV, with its majestic dotted rhythms followed by a fugue, the brilliant
    Praeambulum of Partita V and the Toccata of Partita VI, which frames a long fugue. The largest and
    latest of Bach's keyboard suites, after the English and the French Suites, the Partitas were also
    the most admired in his lifetime.
  }
}

\markup \fill-line {
  \center-column \abs-fontsize #12 \smallCaps \bold {
    \null
    "The dances"
    \vspace #0.6
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold Allemande. A dance of German origin which, by Bach's time, was no longer danced. It is in a
    moderate duple metre, begins with a short upbeat (one or more notes preceding the first
    strong beat of the bar) and unfolds in a continuous flow of semiquavers,
    often shared between the voices in imitation.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold { Courante and Corrente. } Two distinct dances share this name. The French courante
    (Partitas II and IV) is a dignified dance in 3/2, whose accents shift between two groups of three
    beats and three groups of two. This rhythmic device, the hemiola (from the Greek for “one and a
    half”, after the 3:2 ratio), briefly makes a triple metre sound like a duple one. The Italian corrente (Partitas I, III, V and VI) is
    quicker, in 3/4 or 3/8, with running figuration and a simpler texture.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold Sarabande. Born in sixteenth-century Spanish America as a lively and even scandalous dance,
    the zarabanda became in France a slow, solemn dance in triple metre, often with a stress on the
    second beat. It is the expressive centre of the suite, and its melody is often richly ornamented.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold { Menuet, Minuetto. } The most popular court dance of the French Baroque, in a moderate triple
    metre with regular four-bar phrases. Minuets often come in pairs, as in Partita I, where the second
    is played between the first and its repeat. The Tempo di Minuetto of Partita V plays on the
    ambiguity between 3/4 and 6/8.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold Passepied. A dance from Brittany, a quick and light variety of the minuet in 3/8 or 3/4,
    with frequent hemiolas (Partita V).
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold Gavotte. A French dance in duple metre of popular origin, recognisable by its upbeat of half
    a bar. In Partita VI it appears as a Tempo di Gavotta, a freer piece in the character of a gavotte.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold Gigue. Derived from the English and Irish jig, it is the fast closing dance of the suite,
    usually in compound metre (6/8, 12/8) and often written as a fugue whose subject is inverted in
    the second half. The gigue of Partita VI, in duple metre with sharp dotted rhythms, is one of the
    most unusual Bach wrote.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #0.6
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
    \bold { Galanteries and other pieces. } The \bold Rondeau (Partita II) alternates a recurring
    refrain with contrasting episodes, in the French manner; the \bold Capriccio (Partita II) is a free
    and whimsical piece, here a vigorous three-part fugue; the \bold Burlesca and the \bold Scherzo
    (Partita III) are playful, jesting pieces; the \bold Aria (Partita IV) and the \bold Air (Partita VI)
    are not dances but lively pieces in binary form whose name evokes song.
  }
}
